<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.12.2: Writing an Equation Given the Slope and <emphasis effect="italics">y</emphasis>-intercept</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00262</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.12.2: Writing an Equation Given the Slope and y-Intercept</md:title>
    <md:uuid>40f6665e-5398-489e-bad6-dfcbc8f33f68</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Recall that the slope-intercept form of the equation of a line is \(y=mx+b\), where \(m\) is the slope and \(b\) is the \(𝑦\)-intercept. The \(𝑦\)-intercept is the point where the graph crosses the \(𝑦\)-axis. For \(𝑦\)-intercept \(𝑏\), the coordinates of the point are \((0,𝑏)\).</para>
      <para id="para-00002">In the following activity, you will complete a series of questions regarding this information.</para>
      <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00001">
        <para id="para-00003">Log into <link url="https://student.desmos.com">student.desmos.com</link> using the information provided by your teacher to complete the activity.</para>
      </note>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00002">
      <para id="para-00004">Write the equation of a line in slope-intercept form with a slope of \( - \frac{3}{4} \) and a \( y \)-intercept of \( -2 \).</para>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00003">
        <title>Write Equations Given a Slope and \( y \)-Intercept</title>
        <para id="para-00005">We can determine the slope and intercept of a line if the equation is written in slope-intercept form, \( y=mx+b \). Now we will do the reverse—we will start with the slope and \( y \)-intercept and use them to find the equation of the line.</para>
        <section id="sect-00004">
          <title>Example 1 </title>
          <para id="para-00006">Find the equation of a line with slope \( −1 \) and \( y \)-intercept \( (0,−3) \).</para>
          <para id="para-00007">
            <emphasis>Solution</emphasis>
         </para>
          <para id="para-00008"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Name the slope. \( m = -1 \)</para>
          <para id="para-00009"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Name the \( y \)-intercept. \( b = -3 \)</para>
          <para id="para-00010"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Substitute the values into \( y = mx + b \). \( y = -1x + -3 \) \( y = -x-3 \)</para>
          <para id="para-00011">The same information can be picked up from a graph of a line.</para>
        </section>
        <section id="sect-00005">
          <title>Example 2 </title>
          <para id="para-00012">Find the equation of the line shown in the graph.</para>
          <para id="para-00013">
                  <media alt="insert alt text">
                <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_14_05_01.png"/>
              </media>
            
         </para>
          <para id="para-00014">
            <emphasis>Solution</emphasis>
         </para>
          <para id="para-00015"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the slope by counting the rise and run. \( m=\frac{rise}{run} \) From \( (0, -4) \) to \( (3,-2) \), the slope is \( m = \frac{2}{3} \).</para>
          <para id="para-00016"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the \( y \)-intercept. \( y \)-intercept \( (0, -4) \) \( b = -4 \)</para>
          <para id="para-00017"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Substitute the values into \( y = mx + b \). \( y = mx +b \) \( y = \frac{2}{3} x - 4 \)</para>
        </section>
        <note class="try" id="note-00003">
          <title>Try It: Write Equations Given a Slope and \( y \)-Intercept</title>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
            <problem id="prob-00001">
              <para id="para-00018">Write the equation of the line graphed below in slope-intercept form:</para>
              <para id="para-00019">
                          <media alt="This figure has a graph of a straight line on the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y-axes run from negative 8 to 8. ">
                    <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_14_05_02.png"/>
                  </media>
                
             </para>
              <para id="para-00020">Write down your answer, and then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00001">
              <para id="para-00021">Compare your answer: Here is how to write the equation of the line in slope-intercept form.</para>
              <para id="para-00022"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the slope by counting the rise and run. \( m=\frac{rise}{run} \) From \( (0, 1) \) to \( (5, 4) \), the slope is \( m = \frac{3}{5} \).</para>
              <para id="para-00023"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the \( y \)-intercept. \( y \)-intercept \( (0, 1) \) \( b = 1 \)</para>
              <para id="para-00024"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Substitute the values into \( y = mx + b\). \( y = mx +b \) \( y = \frac{3}{5} x + 1 \)</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise></note>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
